WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:05.028
[SOUND] Joel thanks for overly kind words,
and

00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:10.434
thanks for your all for letting me be here
today.

00:00:10.434 --> 00:00:14.816
As Joe mentioned I'm one of about 200 four

00:00:14.816 --> 00:00:20.020
star generals, and I'm sure I'm in the top
196.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.110
I wanna ask you to do something for me
though.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.600
I've gotten to speak since I retired at
some amazing places.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.420
And I went to the UK and spoke at Oxford.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.480
And that's an amazing experience, so I
went in and they took me

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.050
in a historic room, a lot of polished wood
and stuff like that, and

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.810
right before you go in, they show you a
picture of Teddy Roosevelt,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.160
speaking right from where you're gonna
speak,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.350
just to remind you're not Teddy Roosevelt.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.250
So I go in to speak and I've, I've done a
little prep and I think I'm gonna

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.250
do pretty well but, right in front of me,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.900
as soon as I started talking, a person
falls asleep.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.240
And if you've ever tried to speak
publicly, and right

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.447
in front of where your gaze is a person's
almost snoring.

00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:10.360
[LAUGH] It's really distracting, so I
don't think, I haven't been invited back.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.242
So, and that was Annie, my wife.

00:01:11.242 --> 00:01:15.806
[LAUGH] So, if anybody here, could help me
just

00:01:15.806 --> 00:01:19.912
kinda poke her if you see her nodding off.

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:21.390
That, that would be good.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.640
But, you know, she's been special to me,
and as Joel mentioned, you know, go

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:28.700
through a military career there are points
in life that are pretty, pretty big deals.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.430
And for, in the Army when you get selected
for

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:36.560
brigadier general, when you're promoted to
that it's a big step.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.450
Get about 24, 25 years in service.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:38.790
It's a big step.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.070
And it's designed to pump your ego up.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.100
And I was a systems division commander for
an old friend of mine.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.100
So, I'm in this ceremony with my parents

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.520
present, Annie's parents present, a bunch
of friends.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.750
An old friend of mine pins this general
star

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.500
on me, so I am feeling very good about me.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.680
And so, I slide up next to Annie and I go,
Annie in your wildest

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.390
dreams, did you ever think you'd be

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.889
standing next to me as a brigadier
general?

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.060
And

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.335
she said Stan, you're not in my wildest
dreams.

00:02:12.335 --> 00:02:16.542
[LAUGH] So, [SOUND] yeah.

00:02:16.542 --> 00:02:21.472
So, my ego is well in check, and it's,
it's right there, so.

00:02:21.472 --> 00:02:24.138
Thanks thanks very much for letting me be
here.

00:02:24.138 --> 00:02:26.005
What I thought we'd talk about a little
bit today is

00:02:26.005 --> 00:02:28.490
leadership, because it's something that we
all know a lot about.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.810
We all know most of the right answers.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:38.349
But, we as a group, and as a, nation, tend
to struggle getting the answers right.

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:45.810
So, sometimes we gotta set, and look at
very strong statements.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.060
A lot of people say this now.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.400
And if you believe that, whether you do or
not, you'd have to believe this.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:51.900
And

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.150
then we gotta look inside, because we go
okay,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.380
what would cause that, why would that be
the case?

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.390
Or is that really true?

00:02:59.390 --> 00:02:59.949
No, we're great leaders.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.400
But, then you look at some of the things

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.960
that have happened over the last even
decade, some of

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.200
the challenges we've faced and you ask,
whether we've

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.990
as effectively dealt with them as we would
like to.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:16.211
And it's arguable whether we did.

00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:19.191
So you come to the question okay, why?

00:03:19.191 --> 00:03:20.311
Have we gotten stupid?

00:03:20.311 --> 00:03:21.335
Have we gotten lazy?

00:03:21.335 --> 00:03:23.465
Have we gotten selfish?

00:03:23.465 --> 00:03:29.990
What has caused us, to be so concerned
about the level of leadership in America?

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:32.570
And I've got a few thoughts.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.780
First off, I don't think we've gotten
stupid.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:36.820
I don't think we've gotten soft.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.500
I don't think we've gotten of bad
intentions.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.410
I think we still wanna lead well.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.350
But, I think what has happened is we had
many, many years

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.730
in which one model, and one style of
leadership was rewarded with success.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.650
The things that worked well, worked well
for generations, and,

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.220
so they were reinforced by people wanting
to use those more.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.020
And that's natural, you do what works.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.190
But in fact, as things start to change,

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.470
sometimes you find they don't work as
well.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.080
Sometimes you get more competition we do.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.612
As Tom Friedman will tell you about the
world

00:04:09.612 --> 00:04:12.357
being flat it's much closer than it used
to be.

00:04:12.357 --> 00:04:16.320
We're not competing with the people next
door, we're competing with everyone now.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.310
Also things happen faster.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.620
We communicate faster.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.160
It all goes at a speed.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.660
You can't be in a front office and let
things come up

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.250
at the speed of paper, through a
bureaucracy and be relevant anymore.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.340
They're more complex.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.860
They've always been more, they've always
been complex,

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.660
but I'd argue it's probably more than
ever.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.570
And you know when you tell everybody to do
something.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.280
If you're the great leader in the front
office, they don't always

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.030
do it with quite the speed, or dedication
that you wish they were.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.190
The discipline of a lot of things in our
society, look at our political

00:04:54.190 --> 00:05:00.000
parties, don't respond like we think they
did, or like we think they should.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.960
And so what I think we've got now is a
gap.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.060
We had a very successful model, or at

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.250
least it was successful for many people
and suddenly

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.680
we've got a requirement that's different
from what

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.380
we had, and so it's not working so well.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.990
Now this was something that I think I

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:20.210
personally experienced in my career in the
military.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:22.830
And so maybe I can put it in that

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:25.060
context.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.590
For me, where it began.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:28.270
In April of 1980, I was a young

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.760
special forces officer, a Green Beret, in
Thailand.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.546
And thousands of miles away from where I
was,

00:05:33.546 --> 00:05:37.007
something happened that changed the rest
of my career.

00:05:37.007 --> 00:05:38.650
And I had no idea, at the time, that it
would.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.712
And what occurred was, in the deserts of

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:45.858
Iran, a mission called Eagle Claw which
was President

00:05:45.858 --> 00:05:51.087
Carter's directed rescue attempt, to bring
Americans captured,

00:05:51.087 --> 00:05:55.343
held hostage in the American Embassy in
Tehran failed.

00:05:55.343 --> 00:05:57.799
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:57.799 --> 00:06:01.130
And it failed very publicly, and failed
very painfully.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:05.180
What happened was, the raid force went in,
and two groups, it went in in helicopters.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.340
And it went in in C130 aircraft.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.160
And on the ground, the helicopter
maintenance got to the point

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.240
where they had to abort the mission, and
that was disappointing.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.358
Then as they were moving to pull that
force out a C130 fixed wing aircraft

00:06:20.358 --> 00:06:25.639
and a helicopter collided, produced a
fireball, killed eight Americans,

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:30.289
and caused the mission to have to be
aborted of course publicly now.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:38.490
The tragic loss of life, the failure of an
American mission to bring Americans home.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.457
And put this in context for you, this was
about five

00:06:41.457 --> 00:06:45.610
years after America had pulled out of
Vietnam, in great frustration.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.475
And it was four years after Israel had

00:06:48.475 --> 00:06:53.110
pulled off the raid at Entebbe, with
tremendous success.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.100
So what happened was, you had America.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:07:01.646
The forces that I was a part of called, to
do a National mission and end in failure.

00:07:01.646 --> 00:07:04.845
It was extraordinarily important.

00:07:04.845 --> 00:07:06.908
It was extraordinarily important not just,
because what

00:07:06.908 --> 00:07:08.770
happened, but, because what people did
about it.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.070
To a degree I would argue that, had that
mission

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:16.930
been successful, America's special
operations forces now which you read

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.150
and see so much about, wouldn't be what
they are,

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:22.180
because people would have been satisfied
with the status quo.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.980
Instead, what happened in that community
is people said, never again.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.430
We'll never be called on and not be able,
to do

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:33.530
what the nation asks us to do and needs us
to do.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.830
And, so a change began.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.210
And it started by taking some units which
has existed, and some units

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:43.960
which were stood up, very specialized
forces like the Seals, Rangers, et cetera.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.236
And those forces were honed for the
missions that people thought

00:07:48.236 --> 00:07:52.762
were gonna be required, and very quickly
they became world class.

00:07:52.762 --> 00:07:57.466
I mean, they became as good or better than
what anyone had in the world.

00:07:57.466 --> 00:07:59.867
And they became successful in a number of

00:07:59.867 --> 00:08:03.550
places around the world, Panama, Grenada,
other places.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:06.320
They had extraordinary success in doing
what the nation asked.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.232
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:08:12.232 --> 00:08:13.580
But they were also stove-piped.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.020
They tended to grow separately.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.510
When they grew separately, as a lot of
cohesive

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:22.760
organizations do, they tend to become a
bit tribal.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.000
I mean, look at any organization that's
got

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.220
great pride in itself, any team, any
University.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.540
Any element for turning it feels great,
you tend to

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:35.900
become, so cohesive that nobody's quite as
good as you.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.100
So you're not really comfortable dealing
with anybody, but you,

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:45.930
so we had this very effective, but very
tribal organization.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.460
And to solve this problem a headquarters
is created called Joint

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:53.990
Special Operations Command, and this was
to create a team of teams,

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:57.940
to take these organizations and make them
into a cohesive organization that

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.470
could do whatever the nation did, and it
was very, very effective.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:03.979
Extraordinary so, but it was, also,
narrowly focused.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.578
It was designed for hostage rescue.

00:09:07.578 --> 00:09:10.035
It was designed for going after

00:09:10.035 --> 00:09:14.580
people like the Palestinian Liberation
Organization.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.740
Very finite challenges to specific
problems that the nation faced.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.808
And that was fine, until things changed.

00:09:23.808 --> 00:09:30.500
Actually things changed a bit earlier than
we think on 9/11, because although

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:36.270
a lot of people don't realize it, Al Qaeda
declared war on the United States in 1996.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.997
Most Americans didn't know that.

00:09:38.997 --> 00:09:42.350
Most Americans think it began with the
attack on the world trade towers.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.620
On September 11th, when in fact we had a
different challenge in the world.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:51.340
Lots of causes for it, room for about
three more long-term discussions.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.210
But it was a different requirement

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.020
for a force designed for counter-terrorist
operations.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:01.183
Suddenly we had something that was
culturally

00:10:01.183 --> 00:10:04.029
different than what we had faced before.

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:07.950
And it was difficult to understand.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.593
It didn't have a finite doctrine that was
as a clear

00:10:10.593 --> 00:10:14.663
as some of the previous organizations that
we structured ourselves against.

00:10:14.663 --> 00:10:16.293
And we geographically dispersed it.

00:10:16.293 --> 00:10:17.509
It wasn't in one place.

00:10:17.509 --> 00:10:18.860
It was in a bunch of places.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.772
And it moved around all the time.

00:10:20.772 --> 00:10:24.191
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:10:24.191 --> 00:10:26.802
It also had a tendency to change
everything it

00:10:26.802 --> 00:10:29.069
did would be, for a very short period of

00:10:29.069 --> 00:10:31.749
time, if you dealt with Al Qaeda in
associated

00:10:31.749 --> 00:10:37.330
movements one month, three months later,
it was extraordinarily different.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:40.819
And so you had to constantly update your
thinking on how you dealt with it.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.160
And they operated differently.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.000
If you're old enough to remember or read
about the

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.690
terrorist operations in the 1970's and the
1980's, they tended

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:54.430
to be high profile things like hijackings
or hostage taking,

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.930
and then there would be a series of
demands made.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.970
And the demands would say release people

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.540
from prison, and we'll release the
hostages.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.270
Or, put a certain amount of money, or do
something like that.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.510
This was an era in which terrorists didn't
do that.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:13.136
They made a demand like get all Western
forces out of the

00:11:13.136 --> 00:11:19.250
Middle East which was probably ill
illogical.

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:20.330
Or, at least, not gonna happen.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:26.370
But then instead of making hostages and
making demands, they simply kill people.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.357
They crashed aircraft into towers.

00:11:28.357 --> 00:11:31.667
They blew things up and then they looked
you in the

00:11:31.667 --> 00:11:35.555
eye and said, It's a tough world, how do
you like that?

00:11:35.555 --> 00:11:39.170
So very different challenge faced us.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.080
So there's a gap.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.270
There's a very narrow, very effective,
focused capacity

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:49.850
against a very wide, very different kind
of problem.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.550
And it took a different approach to deal
with it.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:57.860
And the approach is probably pretty common
sense.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.870
There were a number of organizations that
had to become part of the solution.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:02.880
They were a whole of government approach.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:06.460
Different intelligent agencies, Department
of State, FBI and what

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:09.590
not, all had to become a part of this.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.265
And partner Nations as well, if we were

00:12:12.265 --> 00:12:15.518
going to understand and deal with the
problem.

00:12:15.518 --> 00:12:20.173
So, that's pretty straightforward, just
get all of these entities, bring

00:12:20.173 --> 00:12:24.345
them into a single team, and go accomplish
a complex, requirement.

00:12:24.345 --> 00:12:28.648
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:12:28.648 --> 00:12:31.420
If you've ever been a part of this, I've
never seen that happen much.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.630
Because, that's just not the way that
people operate.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:40.840
It works on paper even on a key note
slide, but this

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:45.940
took us several years to pull of, because
there were some real challenges.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:48.960
First off, we had a counter terrorist task
force that had all these

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.540
forces in it, and I was the leader, but I
wasn't in command.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.740
I commanded the military part of it, but
everybody else was there on a handshake.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.070
So, everybody else was there basically on
a volunteer basis from

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.600
their organization or individually, so you
can't tell them what to do.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.100
I didn't

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:07.720
have statutory control.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:08.390
I couldn't hire them.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:09.440
I couldn't fire them.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:10.710
I couldn't give them a pay raise.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:11.740
I couldn't give them a bonus.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:13.190
I couldn't do anything like that, so
they're

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.550
there working as part of this task force.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.470
But I don't have the kind of coercive
control, or

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.930
reinforcing things, that we normally

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.210
attach to management or commanding
control.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:28.760
So, it required us to lead by influence.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:32.140
Now, if you think military, you think we
don't lead by influence,

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.330
we lead by tell everybody to do this and
everybody does that.

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:36.559
That's never been the truth in the
military.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:41.270
But in this kind of organization, it was
particularly not gonna be the truth.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:45.920
So, you had to convince people that what
they, you wanted them to

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.940
do was something that they wanted to do,
and it was in their interest.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:54.020
And so, we came upon something that was
very different from what we expected.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:58.087
The real solution to this, was something
we

00:13:58.087 --> 00:14:01.196
came up with, called shared consciousness
and purpose.

00:14:01.196 --> 00:14:07.371
And if you think about, I grew up with
sort of, barrel chested, big knuckled

00:14:07.371 --> 00:14:14.320
commandos, and you throw something like
that up there, you'll get your ass kicked.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:14.464
You know.

00:14:14.464 --> 00:14:14.964
[LAUGH] So.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:20.260
You had to go back and remind them what
it's about, cuz this is what it's about.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.380
It's about winning.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:22.500
And I'm not talking just military.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:25.420
If you're in a business, it's about
winning what you do.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:28.460
If you're an educator it's about educating
kids.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.980
There's some standard of winning in
whatever

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:32.370
you do, and that's what it's about.

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.270
So the first thing if you think about,
with winning, it's results.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.830
You gotta figure out what results you are
and you gotta identify them.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:44.995
In our case, we had to prevent Al Qaeda
attacks.

00:14:44.995 --> 00:14:46.390
The second, we had to be effective.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.122
And that meant, for us, very precise
operations.

00:14:50.122 --> 00:14:53.023
The kind you've gotten used to seeing, the
raid

00:14:53.023 --> 00:14:57.429
that got Osama Bin Laden, extraordinarily
effective in its execution.

00:14:57.429 --> 00:15:02.632
In this case shade would be on the right
target at the right time, every time.

00:15:02.632 --> 00:15:06.902
But suddenly, you had to do this not once
every

00:15:06.902 --> 00:15:11.843
three months, you had to do this, ten
times a night.

00:15:11.843 --> 00:15:13.298
Because it had to be scalable.

00:15:13.298 --> 00:15:15.867
The size of the problem that Al Qaeda and
associated

00:15:15.867 --> 00:15:17.959
movements, gave us was extraordinarily

00:15:17.959 --> 00:15:20.544
different, than anything we'd focused
before.

00:15:20.544 --> 00:15:23.126
We were an organization that was designed,
to be a

00:15:23.126 --> 00:15:26.887
very deliberate in its planning and
execution, been very successful.

00:15:26.887 --> 00:15:30.493
But instead suddenly we had to do that on
a speed, that didn't allow

00:15:30.493 --> 00:15:33.088
you to have centralized control of
operations,

00:15:33.088 --> 00:15:34.813
in the way that you were used to.

00:15:34.813 --> 00:15:38.918
This is one month in Iraq, October, 2007.

00:15:38.918 --> 00:15:43.058
That is just key leaders captured or
killed.

00:15:43.058 --> 00:15:45.874
The scope of violence at that point in
Iraq

00:15:45.874 --> 00:15:49.343
was such that, that level of effort was
required.

00:15:49.343 --> 00:15:54.264
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:15:54.264 --> 00:15:58.290
So how do you get there, how do you do
that?

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.259
The solution's pretty clear.

00:16:01.259 --> 00:16:07.896
In a very complex, constantly changing
environment, you gotta be operating

00:16:07.896 --> 00:16:14.244
at the highest level ever, faster than
anyone ever even thought about it.

00:16:14.244 --> 00:16:18.113
Now how do you get that mission statement
from somebody?

00:16:18.113 --> 00:16:20.060
Do cold fusion by morning.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:23.608
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:16:23.608 --> 00:16:26.334
So what's the, the approach to fixing
this?

00:16:26.334 --> 00:16:27.345
How do you get there?

00:16:27.345 --> 00:16:29.810
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:34.847
This is the first, reflexive way most
organizations, and ours did.

00:16:34.847 --> 00:16:40.105
We're gonna do what we already do, we're
just gonna do it better.

00:16:40.105 --> 00:16:41.499
We're gonna become the best shots, the

00:16:41.499 --> 00:16:43.181
best pilots, the best everything in the
world.

00:16:43.181 --> 00:16:47.717
And we're gonna execute so well, and we're
gonna work harder than ever before.

00:16:47.717 --> 00:16:51.057
The problem with this is there is a limit
to physics.

00:16:51.057 --> 00:16:52.836
You can get better and better and better.

00:16:52.836 --> 00:16:54.602
At a certain point you can't get that much
better.

00:16:54.602 --> 00:16:58.736
The the assembly line can't get that much
more efficient and faster.

00:16:58.736 --> 00:17:02.031
Some other way has got to be found.

00:17:02.031 --> 00:17:04.777
And, this is what we came up with.

00:17:04.777 --> 00:17:09.940
Shared consciousness and purpose, we
didn't put it in a big, fuzzy, red ball.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:11.083
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:17:11.083 --> 00:17:15.053
&gt;&gt; For obvious reasons.

00:17:15.053 --> 00:17:18.020
So, we spring this on people, and this is
great.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:22.664
Cuz, you know, that's the obvious answer,
right?

00:17:22.664 --> 00:17:24.674
And then that's the obvious question.

00:17:24.674 --> 00:17:25.626
I got it boss.

00:17:25.626 --> 00:17:29.010
We'll do that, and oh by the way what is
it?

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.148
So what do we think it is?

00:17:30.148 --> 00:17:34.530
This is what we think it is, or what I
think it is.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:38.217
It's everybody understanding everything.

00:17:38.217 --> 00:17:40.517
Obviously that's an unobtainable standard,
but

00:17:40.517 --> 00:17:42.555
everybody having all the same information.

00:17:42.555 --> 00:17:46.104
Not to draw the same conclusions, but so
that everybody

00:17:46.104 --> 00:17:49.742
has all the pieces of the puzzle and the
combined wisdom.

00:17:49.742 --> 00:17:52.681
Gives you the opportunity not to be
dependent upon a

00:17:52.681 --> 00:17:56.385
single person or, a few people, to direct
this organization.

00:17:56.385 --> 00:17:58.721
And then the second part of it is all
these

00:17:58.721 --> 00:18:02.592
people across the organization, feel like
they own the mission.

00:18:02.592 --> 00:18:04.825
If you ask what people what their mission

00:18:04.825 --> 00:18:07.656
is, it's not, hey I'm here cutting this
stone.

00:18:07.656 --> 00:18:11.494
It's I'm part of a team building a
cathedral.

00:18:11.494 --> 00:18:15.889
And it's really a big change, in many
organizations.

00:18:15.889 --> 00:18:18.275
And it was big change in our organization.

00:18:18.275 --> 00:18:20.836
So we came up with really breaking it into
three parts.

00:18:20.836 --> 00:18:23.941
The first was designing it and that's
understanding what you're doing.

00:18:23.941 --> 00:18:25.533
We'll talk quickly about it, what.

00:18:25.533 --> 00:18:27.555
Executing, and how do you actually do it.

00:18:27.555 --> 00:18:30.680
That's where most organizations, struggle.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.505
And then finally, leading it because
that's

00:18:33.505 --> 00:18:36.480
the most important but the most difficult
part.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.362
So think about defining winning.

00:18:39.362 --> 00:18:42.018
We went to Afghanistan in the fall of
2001.

00:18:42.018 --> 00:18:44.631
We've been there ever since, and yet if I
asked everyone

00:18:44.631 --> 00:18:47.525
in this room what the definition of
winning in Afghanistan is.

00:18:47.525 --> 00:18:51.435
We'd probably get many, many different
answers.

00:18:51.435 --> 00:18:53.554
When I went there in the summer of 2009 to

00:18:53.554 --> 00:18:57.414
take over, I had different mission
statements from different headquarters.

00:18:57.414 --> 00:18:59.638
I was a NATO General, and I was a US
Commander.

00:18:59.638 --> 00:19:02.115
And I had two different mission
statements.

00:19:02.115 --> 00:19:04.791
Which is great, just pick the one you
want.

00:19:04.791 --> 00:19:08.159
You're always gonna to be in trouble.

00:19:08.159 --> 00:19:09.966
So you gotta define winning and then the

00:19:09.966 --> 00:19:12.171
second is, you gotta rely on your strategy
in.

00:19:12.171 --> 00:19:14.626
We had 46 nations in Afghanistan.

00:19:14.626 --> 00:19:17.513
The bottom line is, everybody's gotta
understand not just

00:19:17.513 --> 00:19:20.180
where you're going, but how you're gonna
get there.

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:23.465
And if you think about any organization,
if you don't have

00:19:23.465 --> 00:19:28.144
an agreement on how you're gonna get
there, you're gonna struggle, en-route.

00:19:28.144 --> 00:19:29.526
And then defining team.

00:19:29.526 --> 00:19:32.791
What's your team, for something?

00:19:32.791 --> 00:19:34.780
Typically when we define team we want to
say

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:36.942
it's the people who wear the uniform we
wear.

00:19:36.942 --> 00:19:39.451
It's the people who operate in the same
organization.

00:19:39.451 --> 00:19:42.404
It's the people whose paycheck are signed
by the same person.

00:19:42.404 --> 00:19:45.866
People in the same school, same hat, same
sweatshirt, you name it.

00:19:45.866 --> 00:19:48.623
But I'll argue that's way too small.

00:19:48.623 --> 00:19:53.460
The team is whatever community is required
to get the task done.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:58.456
So if you think about it, the team
required to put this

00:19:58.456 --> 00:20:03.467
talk on today, the business school,
obviously me.

00:20:03.467 --> 00:20:05.695
Somebody had to get me here.

00:20:05.695 --> 00:20:07.962
Somebody had to turn the lights on,
somebody's got to produce

00:20:07.962 --> 00:20:10.420
the power for this, somebody's got to do
all the different parts.

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:12.789
I'd argue that's the team.

00:20:12.789 --> 00:20:16.600
And it's much more complex than you think
and one of the problems is,

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.080
everybody's got to understand they're on
the team, and feel like a part of it.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:24.586
So unlike just the group that is on the
target, all the people, the

00:20:24.586 --> 00:20:27.154
Medevac, the people flying, predator UAVs
and

00:20:27.154 --> 00:20:29.990
whatnot, they're just as important as the
people.

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:32.213
Doing the actual task because if

00:20:32.213 --> 00:20:36.504
they're not successful, the endeavor's not
successful.

00:20:36.504 --> 00:20:40.079
We've got to reward and motivate people as
part of that wider team.

00:20:40.079 --> 00:20:43.723
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:20:43.723 --> 00:20:44.803
Then building a network.

00:20:44.803 --> 00:20:47.232
We talk a lot about building networks and
that's

00:20:47.232 --> 00:20:51.153
building, typically we confuse that with
just the information technology.

00:20:51.153 --> 00:20:53.037
We build connectivity to everyone, we

00:20:53.037 --> 00:20:55.834
give everybody a cellphone, iPad,
computer, whatever

00:20:55.834 --> 00:20:58.988
it is we're gonna use, and we say, okay,
we've got a network.

00:20:58.988 --> 00:21:02.071
Well in reality, you've got the potential
for a network.

00:21:02.071 --> 00:21:05.954
A network is that connection that actually
occurs that does something.

00:21:05.954 --> 00:21:09.022
Do people communicate, do people,
collaborate, do

00:21:09.022 --> 00:21:12.703
people operate more effectively because of
that connectivity?

00:21:12.703 --> 00:21:15.952
And I would say that, that's the goal but
they don't automatically.

00:21:15.952 --> 00:21:19.375
You've gotta foster and force
communication.

00:21:19.375 --> 00:21:21.419
This is a picture of Mike Bloomberg.

00:21:21.419 --> 00:21:23.321
That's where he works in New York City.

00:21:23.321 --> 00:21:26.021
That's a place called a, the bullpen.

00:21:26.021 --> 00:21:29.066
It's a room a little smaller than this,
and he sits in the middle.

00:21:29.066 --> 00:21:30.581
He doesn't have a private office.

00:21:30.581 --> 00:21:32.987
This is a billionaire mayor, of America

00:21:32.987 --> 00:21:35.830
biggest city, and he doesn't have an
office.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:38.274
And you go in and talk to him, you say,
well, where do you really meet people?

00:21:38.274 --> 00:21:39.363
He goes, right here.

00:21:39.363 --> 00:21:40.933
And if people wanna talk to him, they

00:21:40.933 --> 00:21:43.222
talk to him right there, and it passes
information.

00:21:43.222 --> 00:21:47.079
And it's extraordinarily effective.

00:21:47.079 --> 00:21:48.147
There he is.

00:21:48.147 --> 00:21:52.697
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:21:52.697 --> 00:21:54.481
Then, you gotta do what you say is
important.

00:21:54.481 --> 00:21:56.939
We talked about discipline and pret,
prioritizing the effort.

00:21:56.939 --> 00:22:00.456
This is, a tool we used, it's a
synchronization matrix.

00:22:00.456 --> 00:22:02.183
The bottom line is, if you take most

00:22:02.183 --> 00:22:04.546
people's schedule, and you say, first you
ask

00:22:04.546 --> 00:22:08.705
them, what's important to you in your life
or your job, and they, they list it out.

00:22:08.705 --> 00:22:11.655
Then you take their calendar or their
daily schedule

00:22:11.655 --> 00:22:14.482
and said, where do you actually spend your
time?

00:22:14.482 --> 00:22:18.596
And you'll find, we're very, challenged,
to spend

00:22:18.596 --> 00:22:21.929
our time against what we think is
important.

00:22:21.929 --> 00:22:25.084
Similarly our money or anything else we've
got, to

00:22:25.084 --> 00:22:28.464
spend our real assets against what's
important to us.

00:22:28.464 --> 00:22:31.244
And it takes rigorous discipline to make
that occur.

00:22:31.244 --> 00:22:34.284
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:22:34.284 --> 00:22:37.577
Then you gotta lead this thing.

00:22:37.577 --> 00:22:38.976
You gotta build relationships.

00:22:38.976 --> 00:22:41.964
There's a picture here, of two gentlemen.

00:22:41.964 --> 00:22:48.318
President LeClerc, obviously, of South
Africa, and Nelson Mandela.

00:22:48.318 --> 00:22:50.936
Now they had 2 organizations.

00:22:50.936 --> 00:22:55.331
A white apartheid government, and the
African National Congress, and they

00:22:55.331 --> 00:22:59.400
were the leaders of organizations that
didn't want to come together.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:03.066
Those two organizations were polarized,
and it was producing violence

00:23:03.066 --> 00:23:05.541
and it was likely to produce a lot more
violence.

00:23:05.541 --> 00:23:09.462
So you had 2 leaders, who had to make the
personal decision.

00:23:09.462 --> 00:23:11.769
To pull themselves into a personal

00:23:11.769 --> 00:23:15.589
relationship, at great risk to themselves
politically

00:23:15.589 --> 00:23:17.975
and even physically, so that their

00:23:17.975 --> 00:23:21.409
organizations would be pulled toward the
center.

00:23:21.409 --> 00:23:24.315
Now my mind that's leadership right there.

00:23:24.315 --> 00:23:27.134
Because they led not only their
organizations they led

00:23:27.134 --> 00:23:30.387
the nation, but they did it through a
personal relationship.

00:23:30.387 --> 00:23:34.696
And these are not two guys who met in a
sports bar, and hung out.

00:23:34.696 --> 00:23:38.857
One had to let the other out of prison, so
that they could build a relationship.

00:23:38.857 --> 00:23:43.949
Extraordinarily, challenging, but
extraordinarily important.

00:23:43.949 --> 00:23:48.323
Something that I'm very interested in is
the, the American Civil Rights Movement.

00:23:48.323 --> 00:23:52.146
My family's from the South, and I got to
watch it as a boy.

00:23:52.146 --> 00:23:54.366
And it was an extraordinary strategy.

00:23:54.366 --> 00:23:56.955
This is one of the few movements that ever

00:23:56.955 --> 00:24:02.527
followed a disciplined strategy, of
non-violent protests, that was successful.

00:24:02.527 --> 00:24:04.972
And it was a great strategy, and it was
disciplined for people.

00:24:04.972 --> 00:24:07.581
But it didn't succeed just because of
that.

00:24:07.581 --> 00:24:09.868
It took an inspirational leader.

00:24:09.868 --> 00:24:11.539
In fact, leaders.

00:24:11.539 --> 00:24:14.874
We think of Martin Luther King, but
they're actually more than, one person.

00:24:14.874 --> 00:24:17.953
But it took extraordinarily inspirational
leaders, who were

00:24:17.953 --> 00:24:19.876
willing to stand up and take that role.

00:24:19.876 --> 00:24:25.047
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:24:25.047 --> 00:24:29.093
People who are willing to realize that
leaders actually lead.

00:24:29.093 --> 00:24:33.863
They don't follow opinion.

00:24:33.863 --> 00:24:36.126
And there's the requirement to change.

00:24:36.126 --> 00:24:38.171
In combat, if you don't change, you pay
price.

00:24:38.171 --> 00:24:40.162
If you're at Liebman Brothers, and you
don't

00:24:40.162 --> 00:24:41.767
pay a price, you get a cardboard box.

00:24:41.767 --> 00:24:44.708
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:24:44.708 --> 00:24:49.661
So, the organization either learns, and
adapts, or it's gone.

00:24:49.661 --> 00:24:52.252
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:24:52.252 --> 00:24:53.380
So, what is this?

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:55.189
It's shared consciousness and purpose.

00:24:55.189 --> 00:24:57.902
This is, it's not a cycle that happens in

00:24:57.902 --> 00:25:01.944
sequence, it's a height, cycle that
happens all the time.

00:25:01.944 --> 00:25:05.817
At the heart of it, it's communication.

00:25:05.817 --> 00:25:11.423
Without a doubt, it's more than
communication, it's really leadership.

00:25:11.423 --> 00:25:13.205
And then in reality and this is what

00:25:13.205 --> 00:25:15.671
scares people when you actually talk about
it.

00:25:15.671 --> 00:25:16.618
It's culture change.

00:25:16.618 --> 00:25:18.919
Because why do people, form a culture?

00:25:18.919 --> 00:25:21.092
Because it's comfortable and for them it
works.

00:25:21.092 --> 00:25:23.225
And why do they defend it?

00:25:23.225 --> 00:25:26.612
Because it's worked for them.

00:25:26.612 --> 00:25:28.717
So now as we go forward to what we call

00:25:28.717 --> 00:25:32.311
this now is CrossLead, but it's really
some common sense.

00:25:32.311 --> 00:25:34.948
Forms of leadership.

00:25:34.948 --> 00:25:37.546
Now I want to talk just about a few
leadership intangibles.

00:25:37.546 --> 00:25:39.003
Then we'll open it up for discussion.

00:25:39.003 --> 00:25:43.264
Because, there's an awful lot of this that
is enduring.

00:25:43.264 --> 00:25:45.538
But it's worth talking about.

00:25:45.538 --> 00:25:48.701
That's my only, Rowan poet quote.

00:25:48.701 --> 00:25:50.835
It's the only one I know.

00:25:50.835 --> 00:25:54.489
But it's also important because if you
think about leadership.

00:25:54.489 --> 00:25:59.601
Think about people who've actually
experienced it, and had challenges.

00:25:59.601 --> 00:26:01.282
If someone cruised on to success from

00:26:01.282 --> 00:26:03.672
birth, they're probably not going to have
learned

00:26:03.672 --> 00:26:07.111
as much along the way as someone who's had
to climb over a number of things.

00:26:07.111 --> 00:26:09.776
In business, politics, you name it.

00:26:09.776 --> 00:26:10.983
So let's talk about some of these.

00:26:10.983 --> 00:26:12.772
First is your personal interactions.

00:26:12.772 --> 00:26:15.939
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:26:15.939 --> 00:26:20.148
You are, as a leader, extraordinarily
important.

00:26:20.148 --> 00:26:21.033
You don't think of it.

00:26:21.033 --> 00:26:23.401
We're not raised to have big egos.

00:26:23.401 --> 00:26:26.217
When I was young, I never thought, I
thought of myself as

00:26:26.217 --> 00:26:29.109
any other of the knuckleheads, and I was,
and I still am.

00:26:29.109 --> 00:26:30.785
But when I was a battalion commander I

00:26:30.785 --> 00:26:32.844
used to write personal notes, and one
time.

00:26:32.844 --> 00:26:34.821
I wrote a note to some wives, in the

00:26:34.821 --> 00:26:38.029
unit, that Andy had recommend I do because
they'd done

00:26:38.029 --> 00:26:40.212
a lot of good work for it, and went over

00:26:40.212 --> 00:26:43.238
to their quarters, and one of the notes
was framed.

00:26:43.238 --> 00:26:45.404
It was a handwritten note, just a thank
you note,

00:26:45.404 --> 00:26:47.532
and they had it framed on the wall, the
wife did.

00:26:47.532 --> 00:26:52.662
And you suddenly realize, how important
thanking people are,

00:26:52.662 --> 00:26:56.275
is, and how important you as a leader can
be.

00:26:56.275 --> 00:26:58.124
You can have that impact.

00:26:58.124 --> 00:27:00.071
It doesn't mean you think you're a cool
person.

00:27:00.071 --> 00:27:03.559
But it does mean, you can touch people,
and you should do that.

00:27:03.559 --> 00:27:06.199
So every time you have interactions,
you've gotta

00:27:06.199 --> 00:27:08.119
figure out, how can I get this right?

00:27:08.119 --> 00:27:10.694
And you may have 150 in a day.

00:27:10.694 --> 00:27:14.526
And if you're a senior leader, for me, the
litmus test I always told people was.

00:27:14.526 --> 00:27:17.725
If you talk to someone and they're gonna
go home that night

00:27:17.725 --> 00:27:22.050
and tell their spouse about the
conversation, then you're a senior leader.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:26.594
And every comment you make to someone
matters because if you're, flip, or you're

00:27:26.594 --> 00:27:29.282
tired, or you're cranky or whatever,
they're

00:27:29.282 --> 00:27:31.534
gonna go home and tell their spouse that.

00:27:31.534 --> 00:27:34.977
But if you're positive and upbeat and, and
do the kinds

00:27:34.977 --> 00:27:38.649
of things you wanna do as a leader,
they're gonna do that.

00:27:38.649 --> 00:27:40.225
And that's what you want to be.

00:27:40.225 --> 00:27:41.805
But your interactions are hard.

00:27:41.805 --> 00:27:44.540
I remember standing next to the, the
hospital bed in

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:47.722
the center and that young Afghan soldier
had lost both legs.

00:27:47.722 --> 00:27:48.510
And guess what?

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:50.589
He's not gonna to get the kind of

00:27:50.589 --> 00:27:54.064
medical care there, that we give our
veterans here.

00:27:54.064 --> 00:27:58.494
He gets, pretty good medical care but then
he goes back out into society.

00:27:58.494 --> 00:28:00.869
He doesn't get the kind of prosthesis that
we

00:28:00.869 --> 00:28:03.440
would give our forces cuz they can't
afford it.

00:28:03.440 --> 00:28:05.276
This kid asked me if he could, if we could

00:28:05.276 --> 00:28:07.394
get him legs so he could go back into the
fight.

00:28:07.394 --> 00:28:12.105
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:28:12.105 --> 00:28:16.107
So your personal interactions, not only
affect

00:28:16.107 --> 00:28:20.022
them, but if you're human, they affect
you.

00:28:20.022 --> 00:28:22.538
Then there's time and energy management,
you've only got a certain amount.

00:28:22.538 --> 00:28:25.512
These are two brothers that worked for me
for a number of years.

00:28:25.512 --> 00:28:27.426
This is their typical pose.

00:28:27.426 --> 00:28:30.628
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:28:30.628 --> 00:28:34.915
There's only so much of you, and you can't
create any more.

00:28:34.915 --> 00:28:38.078
You can't drink energy drink and get three
more hours out of the day.

00:28:38.078 --> 00:28:41.172
You can try that for a while, but pretty
soon what you'll

00:28:41.172 --> 00:28:44.152
find is you run out and the people around
you fall down.

00:28:44.152 --> 00:28:45.874
So suddenly you gotta do a couple things.

00:28:45.874 --> 00:28:49.270
One, you gotta realize you're human, and
the second,

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:52.397
you gotta realize that people around you
are human.

00:28:52.397 --> 00:28:56.029
And you gotta take care of them just like
you take care of yourself.

00:28:56.029 --> 00:28:59.528
Obviously I did a good job with them.

00:28:59.528 --> 00:29:01.459
You're gonna work up and out as well.

00:29:01.459 --> 00:29:05.205
We all get very comfortable dealing with
the people that work for us.

00:29:05.205 --> 00:29:09.338
Because they typically are gonna be,
cheerier and more pleasant to

00:29:09.338 --> 00:29:12.540
us because we pay them or, we rate them or
whatever.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.403
But you're gonna have to work up and out.

00:29:14.403 --> 00:29:17.823
In many cases, as you get more senior, the
most important thing you

00:29:17.823 --> 00:29:21.066
do for people below you is to work well
with the people above you.

00:29:21.066 --> 00:29:22.284
And you owe it to 'em.

00:29:22.284 --> 00:29:24.940
You owe it to your, bosses and your peers

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:29.018
to keep them informed, to develop the kind
of relationship.

00:29:29.018 --> 00:29:31.267
But every once in a while, you owe it to
them to

00:29:31.267 --> 00:29:34.478
just reach up, and say hey, thanks, you're
doing a good job.

00:29:34.478 --> 00:29:37.813
And we think of that as being a sycophant,
or sucking up.

00:29:37.813 --> 00:29:42.239
No, sometimes it's just senior people
supporting each other,

00:29:42.239 --> 00:29:46.752
because if, unless you're not human,
you're gonna need that.

00:29:46.752 --> 00:29:50.495
People ask me, when do I lead by example?

00:29:50.495 --> 00:29:54.475
I'll tell you you're leading by example
every moment of every day.

00:29:54.475 --> 00:29:57.678
Either a good example or a bad example,
but you are.

00:29:57.678 --> 00:30:00.818
People watch everything you do.

00:30:00.818 --> 00:30:02.605
They don't watch just when you're up on

00:30:02.605 --> 00:30:04.876
stage giving the talk they watch
everything else.

00:30:04.876 --> 00:30:07.518
If you double park your car out there when
it's

00:30:07.518 --> 00:30:10.294
not allowed, come in give a great talk,
and then

00:30:10.294 --> 00:30:13.411
other people say that, that's gonna get
out there and

00:30:13.411 --> 00:30:16.688
pretty soon it's gonna be that sucker who
double parks.

00:30:16.688 --> 00:30:19.781
Hugely important.

00:30:19.781 --> 00:30:21.353
The guy on the left, I was talking to

00:30:21.353 --> 00:30:23.345
a British NCO he'd just gone into the
Helmand

00:30:23.345 --> 00:30:25.915
River valley December, 2009, had lost his
brother a

00:30:25.915 --> 00:30:27.990
few months prior in Iraq when I was there.

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:29.630
So, as you're dealing with people like
that,

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:35.800
you're showing them, not just that you
care, but

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.820
you're showing you're willing to be in
helmet with them.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:41.280
You're willing to be there when it's late,
when it's dangerous, when

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:44.760
it's cold, whatever it is people don't
like, you're willing to do that.

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:48.610
And in inclusion,

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:51.920
what's most important?

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:58.492
I played junior varsity basketball when I
was in high school at one point.

00:30:58.492 --> 00:31:02.636
And, I was like the seventh guy on about a
10 or 12 man team.

00:31:02.636 --> 00:31:05.378
But, he only played about six guys.

00:31:05.378 --> 00:31:11.896
So the whole year I got in a game one
time, for about 20 seconds.

00:31:11.896 --> 00:31:13.326
I got a shot off and I hit it.

00:31:13.326 --> 00:31:17.577
I had the highest shooting percentage in
the team.

00:31:17.577 --> 00:31:22.814
I haven't gotten any phone calls asking me
to the NBA, but, but the funny thing about

00:31:22.814 --> 00:31:27.792
it is, when we drove to games I wasn't
excited, cuz I knew I wasn't gonna play.

00:31:27.792 --> 00:31:32.910
And when we won, I wasn't excited or happy
and when we lost I wasn't upset.

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.843
Because I wasn't invested in it like I
wanted to be.

00:31:35.843 --> 00:31:36.630
I wanted to be desperately.

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.640
I wanted to have those feelings, but I
didn't.

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:43.196
Now think about it in your organization if
you don't share information if you

00:31:43.196 --> 00:31:48.470
don't make people feel a part of it, are
they gonna celebrate the big win?

00:31:48.470 --> 00:31:50.970
I don't think not unless they are
different from what I was.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:55.510
It's extraordinarily important to make
people feel like they are part of

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:58.050
the whole and there are a lot of ways you
can do it.

00:31:58.050 --> 00:31:59.744
But the first thing is just tell them.

00:31:59.744 --> 00:32:00.573
Just bring them in.

00:32:00.573 --> 00:32:01.161
Just trust them.

00:32:01.161 --> 00:32:05.140
Just give them something.

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:10.600
And then the final is really leadership
during crisis.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:11.810
If you haven't had any crisis, hang on.

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:12.170
They're coming.

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:14.630
You are gonna face some.

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:18.360
And the problem with the crisis is not
that, it's not like

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:22.360
the movie where the crisis arises and you
know exactly what to do.

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:23.570
If that happens, it's not a crisis.

00:32:23.570 --> 00:32:25.884
It's the crisis when it arises and you

00:32:25.884 --> 00:32:29.000
go, holy smoke, this has never happened
before.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.850
What do we do?

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:31.820
And everybody looks at you.

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:35.120
And that's the first thing that happens.

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:35.780
Everybody looks at you.

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:42.583
The picture in the center, this was when
an American jet, at the request of

00:32:42.583 --> 00:32:45.913
a coalition force, dropped two bombs on
some

00:32:45.913 --> 00:32:49.696
fuel trucks that'd been hijacked by the
Taliban.

00:32:49.696 --> 00:32:53.779
The problem is they were surrounded by
civilians, when they dropped em.

00:32:53.779 --> 00:32:57.236
And about 100 civilians were killed.

00:32:57.236 --> 00:32:59.951
Nobody tried to do the wrong thing.

00:32:59.951 --> 00:33:02.317
There's no evil.

00:33:02.317 --> 00:33:04.113
But about a 100 civilians were killed.

00:33:04.113 --> 00:33:07.319
So we suddenly had a crisis with the
Afghan people.

00:33:07.319 --> 00:33:09.074
We had a crisis with the Afghan president.

00:33:09.074 --> 00:33:11.946
We had a crisis with the coalition partner
who had dropped it

00:33:11.946 --> 00:33:13.497
because they were upset and they

00:33:13.497 --> 00:33:16.039
were worried about recriminations on
themselves.

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:20.300
We had levels of crises, so what do you

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:24.380
do?

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:26.300
What I did was, the first thing I did was

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:30.750
try to, across the force, communicate
what's the right thing.

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:34.970
I called President Karzai, I went on TV
and apologized to the Afghan people.

00:33:38.270 --> 00:33:41.020
I dealt with the Coalition Force as openly
as I

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:43.130
could, but I said we're gonna do the right
thing here.

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:44.870
We're not gonna try to hide it.

00:33:44.870 --> 00:33:49.660
We're not gonna try to shape that it
wasn't what it is, and the

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:55.290
whole time you try to be the example cuz
everybody's watching how you respond.

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.680
Everybody watches, your body language,
whether

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.920
you're calm, whether you're not, and

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:05.150
whether you operate from a moral compass
that they wanna believe in.

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:09.150
Cuz as soon as they see you, cutting
corners or shaving

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:14.130
edges, your regard, even if you get away
with it, is gone.

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:14.630
So this is

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:19.280
what I came to believe, people ask me
often whether

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:22.370
leadership is, something that's born with
or whether it's taught.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:24.770
I think you can teach it, the biggest
thing is people just

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:29.720
have to make a decision to be a leader
because the

00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:34.581
thing about leadership, is it's not that
complex, but it's really hard.

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:37.460
I can tell you all the right answers to be
a

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:39.770
leader and you won't write them down
because you'll know them all.

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:43.770
But it's like knowing the right answer to
be in great physical

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:48.030
shape or to be very well read or to speak
six languages.

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:50.290
The difference is just doing it.

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:52.600
And that's where our leadership falls.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:53.957
Thanks, and I'll open it to your
questions.

00:34:53.957 --> 00:35:03.957
[NOISE].

00:35:08.939 --> 00:35:10.239
Sir?

00:35:10.239 --> 00:35:11.704
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is, [UNKNOWN].

00:35:11.704 --> 00:35:13.143
I'm an MBA 1.

00:35:13.143 --> 00:35:17.020
I wanted to ask you about, you mentioned
time and energy management.

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:20.610
And I wanted to ask you about that in the
context of your own, personal routine.

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:22.700
Some of us were talking before the talk.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:26.670
We've read that you only need four hours
of rest

00:35:26.670 --> 00:35:29.550
each night, that you eat one meal a day so
that

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:34.020
you don't feel sluggish, and that you run,
depending on the

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:34.842
accounts we read 8 or 10 or 12 miles a
day.

00:35:34.842 --> 00:35:38.670
[LAUGH] And and we even, one of us read
that you went running with David Petraeus

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:42.090
a couple years ago and he learned that

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:44.260
he wasn't the fastest person at that army
base.

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:47.390
And so what I wanted to ask was, you know
what advice did

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:50.450
you give to your, the officers who worked
for you and you would give

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:54.120
for us even though most of us wont be
going into combat situations

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:58.350
about staying fit and healthy and prepared
to take on the task of leading.

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:01.400
How we structure our days so that we don't
waste time

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:03.810
and get the most out of every moment in
each day?

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:05.130
&gt;&gt; That's a, that's really great.

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:08.760
First thing is you should hire my

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:09.875
publicist who comes up with all that
stuff.

00:36:09.875 --> 00:36:12.180
[LAUGH] no, the reality isn't, you know,
be very,

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:14.130
since I'm among friends here, I'll be very
honest.

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.610
yeah, I eat one meal a day, and that's
just

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:18.880
cuz I do feel sluggish if I eat more than
that.

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:22.322
Now, that meal is a prodigious proportion.

00:36:22.322 --> 00:36:27.385
[LAUGH] It's a, I describe it as anything
I can reach until I fall asleep.

00:36:27.385 --> 00:36:30.834
So, you know, it's just a habit I got in
many,

00:36:30.834 --> 00:36:35.278
many years ago, and so, it's just one of
those things.

00:36:35.278 --> 00:36:37.654
In terms of the amount of sleep, I slept

00:36:37.654 --> 00:36:40.691
about that, because that's how much time I
had.

00:36:40.691 --> 00:36:42.989
If I had more time, I'd be happy to sleep
more.

00:36:42.989 --> 00:36:45.590
And I, you know, I certainly, when I get
the chance, I do.

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:48.320
But I did find out that that's about the
balance that

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:52.110
I needed to, at that point, to do the
things I did.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:54.764
And so, what I tried to do was hit a
balance that made me feel right.

00:36:54.764 --> 00:36:56.625
I got up early in the morning and worked
out,

00:36:56.625 --> 00:36:58.751
cuz that was important for me to get the
day going.

00:36:58.751 --> 00:37:01.575
Then I had some quiet time alone to work.

00:37:01.575 --> 00:37:03.590
Then I did most of the day I'd visit
troops and all.

00:37:03.590 --> 00:37:07.218
And then in the evenings, I'd start to
fade in, in energy.

00:37:07.218 --> 00:37:09.349
And so what I learned to do was put those

00:37:09.349 --> 00:37:13.156
things that don't require analytical
thought, later in the day.

00:37:13.156 --> 00:37:15.749
I mean, guys would learn not, my staff
would learn

00:37:15.749 --> 00:37:18.419
not to come to me for really good
decisions at night.

00:37:18.419 --> 00:37:19.546
But I could sign stuff.

00:37:19.546 --> 00:37:21.996
I mean, I could do, you know, those kinds
of things.

00:37:23.022 --> 00:37:24.565
yeah.

00:37:24.565 --> 00:37:26.412
I mean, you gotta know your limitations.

00:37:27.445 --> 00:37:31.730
But I also learned to have people around
me that really understood.

00:37:31.730 --> 00:37:33.802
I was very transparent about my batter

00:37:33.802 --> 00:37:36.586
rhythm, my strengths and weaknesses, that
could

00:37:36.586 --> 00:37:39.904
fill in the gaps but could also know when
I'm best in the day.

00:37:39.904 --> 00:37:40.835
How much sleep I need.

00:37:40.835 --> 00:37:42.323
I had a certain, you know, guy I

00:37:42.323 --> 00:37:44.705
worked with that just was very protective
of that.

00:37:44.705 --> 00:37:46.880
At times he'd say stop it, go to bed.

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:49.160
You know, that sort of thing is important.

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:53.970
I think all those things are really
important on a couple levels.

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:55.890
One, when you're in an organization,
people want to

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:57.900
know that you are somebody that they have
respect for.

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:03.120
I think if your life is, if your health is
in a shambles, if your

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:08.610
finances are in a shambles, if your
personal life is in a shambles.

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:14.420
You're not gonna build as much confidence
in them, as if you got your act together.

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:16.670
So, it's just like, if they see you cheat

00:38:16.670 --> 00:38:20.150
on their tax, on your taxes and they
wonder

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:21.700
how you're gonna deal with them in a
business

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:24.960
sense, they're gonna do the comp, you
know, the calculation.

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:27.810
If he lies to the US government, he might
lie to me, too.

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:28.900
All those things matter.

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:31.880
And I think you don't need to make a big
publicity about it.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:35.930
People will watch you enough to know
whether you're [UNKNOWN] but

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:38.730
it's not something just for you, you owe
it to them.

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:41.020
Thanks.

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:43.140
&gt;&gt; Hi sir.

00:38:43.140 --> 00:38:45.569
Rob Simmons class of 98 [UNKNOWN] C2.

00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:48.650
I'm here in the Ed school and thinking a
lot

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:53.070
about unit readiness, and how leaders
assess their, their collective units.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:56.230
And, especially under circumstances where
they're doing something new.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:59.750
Like, a new product or a new development
or an air case, you know?

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:03.070
New Afghan unit, getting, ready to do
something.

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:04.490
And kind of, what are the metrics and what
are

00:39:04.490 --> 00:39:08.670
your thinking, around that, that, that we
could benefit from?

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:11.233
&gt;&gt; And I don't know if you'll benefit from
[UNKNOWN].

00:39:11.233 --> 00:39:11.723
Thanks.

00:39:11.723 --> 00:39:16.180
The the key thing about metrics is we tend
to measure what's easy to measure, and

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.100
we tend to struggle what, with measuring
the

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:23.010
things that really tell us what we want
to.

00:39:23.010 --> 00:39:25.240
What you're trying to measure in the new
organization is can

00:39:25.240 --> 00:39:30.840
it do what the new organization is, is
designed for, and so

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:33.800
until you get a chance to use it, you're
trying to create

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:36.440
the analog of that and how many ways can
you measure it?

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:38.680
In military units we would measure how
much equipment

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:41.570
they had, how much, how many senior
leaders, did they

00:39:41.570 --> 00:39:43.150
have the right number of people, did they
have the

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:46.130
right ranks, did they have the right
amount of equipment.

00:39:46.130 --> 00:39:49.390
It tells you some stuff but it's a
predictor but it's not

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:55.710
any guarantee that that organization can
function, and so obviously what I would

00:39:55.710 --> 00:39:59.270
do is, I would you know, those basic ones
are pretty empirical, I

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:03.440
would then try to develop some test which
tell me how they do.

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:05.670
The first would be cohesion though.

00:40:05.670 --> 00:40:09.630
Does that organization, can it do stuff
together?

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:11.346
And so to a degree, even if the

00:40:11.346 --> 00:40:15.007
organization's task was to do something
you can't measure.

00:40:15.007 --> 00:40:18.260
Let's say if their task is to put out
forest fires, but

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:21.443
you can't go light forest fires to check
if they can do

00:40:21.443 --> 00:40:24.691
it, I'd go, well you can, but but I would
do is,

00:40:24.691 --> 00:40:28.264
I would say, what do you really need to
fight a forest fire.

00:40:28.264 --> 00:40:29.990
You need people that are pretty physically
fit.

00:40:29.990 --> 00:40:31.930
You need people who understand the science
of it

00:40:31.930 --> 00:40:33.880
and you need people who work together as a
team.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:38.580
And if I had to go find something that
requires them to work together

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:41.910
as a team, and maybe think a little bit in
the process, I'd do that.

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:44.750
Some physical things that you know,
[INAUDIBLE] team

00:40:44.750 --> 00:40:46.730
sports I think would be very, very good.

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:49.720
For more complex things in business,
you're on your own.

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:51.610
Thanks.

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:58.518
&gt;&gt; Thank you General for coming my name is
[INAUDIBLE] and I'm curious about what

00:40:58.518 --> 00:41:03.335
actions do you think the US and Pakistan
can take to make Pakistan a safer place.

00:41:03.335 --> 00:41:04.377
&gt;&gt; That's a great one.

00:41:04.377 --> 00:41:07.280
if, if you go back and you look at the
history

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:13.134
between the United States and Pakistan, of
course Pakistan formed in 1947.

00:41:13.134 --> 00:41:14.941
It's been uneven.

00:41:14.941 --> 00:41:18.919
There's been a series of periods where
Pakistan felt as though they were

00:41:18.919 --> 00:41:23.481
the better partner for America during the
Cold War, during the 1980s when they

00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:28.369
were the conduit for support to the
Mujaheddin, during 1970 when the Henry

00:41:28.369 --> 00:41:31.239
Kissinger went through Pakistan in
preparation for

00:41:31.239 --> 00:41:33.490
the China trip it's 71, I'm sorry.

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:38.070
Preparation for the China trip that Nixon
took.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:42.560
Pakistan felt like they had done more than
America was doing in return.

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:44.430
And then you have, it goes turnabout, and

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:49.270
their periers where America thinks that,
that America's

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:51.540
doing more and Pakistan is supporting the
Taliban

00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:55.170
or supporting las kuri type or you name
it.

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:58.740
So this huge amount of mistrust, I think
the most important

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:01.649
thing we can do is, everybody step back
and do a reset.

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:07.130
People think what's the most important
part of our relationship with

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:10.020
Pakistan and that's Al Qaeda and I would
argue it's not.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:11.390
Al Qaeda matters.

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:14.510
But if you look at Pakistan's strategic
interests,

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:17.450
which are security from India in the
region.

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:19.890
It's economy, it's management of water
which

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:22.340
is a problem, it's management of
electricity.

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:23.930
And the fact that Pakistan had, has

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:27.718
never had a civilian to civilian
government transfer.

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:32.990
It's always was it, of 64 years of
history,

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:36.250
32 have been military leadership, 32 have
been civilian.

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:42.720
What Pakistan needs is help shaping itself
for its big, strategic challenges

00:42:42.720 --> 00:42:45.570
in the world, and I, I like to believe
American can be a

00:42:45.570 --> 00:42:51.370
partner for that, because America benefits
from the the idea

00:42:51.370 --> 00:42:56.600
of Pakistan being what is it, 117 million
people as a stable nation in that region.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:58.500
So I think we've gotta raise that
discussion

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:01.138
a little bit higher to the strategic
level.

00:43:01.138 --> 00:43:04.750
And we gotta stop poking each other in the
eye on some of the more tactical things.

00:43:04.750 --> 00:43:08.240
There are different tactical objectives.

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:10.800
But the biggest thing is trust.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:13.270
I mean, we are at this point now where the

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:18.120
deficit of trust makes everything hard,
and if, if we

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:21.090
could work on one thing before we work on,
on

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.290
anything else it would just be trust over
and over.

00:43:23.290 --> 00:43:25.590
I know it sounds simple, but it's like
leadership.

00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:28.610
Answer's simple, executing it's really
hard.

00:43:28.610 --> 00:43:28.960
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:30.850
Ma'am.

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:32.370
&gt;&gt; Hi, thank you so much for being

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:35.450
here today, General McChrystal, my name's
Rachel Burleigh.

00:43:35.450 --> 00:43:39.380
I'm, am a PHd student in political science
here and

00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:42.340
I actually had a little bit of a chance to
observe

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:45.340
some of your great work when I was at
special operations

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:46.950
through intensity conflict working on

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:50.240
Afghan reconstruction under Colonel Dallas
Brown.

00:43:50.240 --> 00:43:54.640
But, I had a, a kind of more conceptual
question to ask you, going back

00:43:54.640 --> 00:43:56.040
to one of the final themes of

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:59.430
your presentation on the importance of
cultural change.

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:02.300
And so, I wanted to push you a little bit
more on that and

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:04.290
to say, when your thinking about cultural

00:44:04.290 --> 00:44:06.690
change that's not just between
individuals, who you're

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:11.260
not just talking about leading by example,
but you're really talking about kind of

00:44:11.260 --> 00:44:14.280
you know, group level changes across
groups

00:44:14.280 --> 00:44:17.740
that you can't influence or interact with
individually.

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:19.210
How do you think you help to

00:44:19.210 --> 00:44:22.870
bring about reflection and really
fundamental cultural change?

00:44:22.870 --> 00:44:24.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, great Rachel that's tough.

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:27.560
I really got Joe Felter here, you know
more about it than I ever will.

00:44:27.560 --> 00:44:31.710
Joe ran the counterinsurgency advise and
assist team in Afghanistan.

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:34.681
I just listen to him, so that which went
well, I get credit for.

00:44:34.681 --> 00:44:37.326
That which doesn't, there's a scapegoat.

00:44:37.326 --> 00:44:41.858
No, and that, that is a leadership maxim I
would offer you all.

00:44:41.858 --> 00:44:45.904
The first thing you do in any endeavor is,
designate a scapegoat.

00:44:45.904 --> 00:44:49.833
You know, make sure it's not you.

00:44:49.833 --> 00:44:51.967
But, and it's good if you tell them in
advance.

00:44:51.967 --> 00:44:52.415
So.

00:44:52.415 --> 00:44:53.840
[LAUGH].

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:55.764
No, cultural change is huge.

00:44:55.764 --> 00:44:57.089
And it's easy for me to say.

00:44:57.089 --> 00:44:59.680
The problem with culture is they're there
for a reason.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:02.225
Look at the U.S. civil rights movement.

00:45:02.225 --> 00:45:05.293
When the case of giving all Americans
their rights, it

00:45:05.293 --> 00:45:09.040
wasn't just a case of okay, we'll give
everybody their rights.

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:13.210
The problem is that comes at someone
elses' expense.

00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:17.940
Whether it's really them giving up money
or something, no it's giving up...

00:45:17.940 --> 00:45:20.410
Some people in the South perceived that
they were going

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:25.720
to lose their position in society if
everybody had equal opportunity.

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:28.050
And so you have this great: why should I
buy

00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:31.590
into cultural change if my life's going to
be harder?

00:45:31.590 --> 00:45:35.990
Or my part of whatever it is is I'm
interested is gonna be less?

00:45:35.990 --> 00:45:41.047
And you have to convince people that, one,
it's typically not a zero sum game.

00:45:41.047 --> 00:45:44.197
The place can be better, and I think over
time people accept

00:45:44.197 --> 00:45:47.228
that, but, but near term they have a tough
time with it.

00:45:47.228 --> 00:45:48.929
And you have to make a very cogent

00:45:48.929 --> 00:45:51.890
argument that says you won't be effective
otherwise.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:54.718
When we made the argument in Afghanistan
to, to really

00:45:54.718 --> 00:45:59.082
focus on counter-insurgency, we didn't get
everybody together and say: guys,

00:45:59.082 --> 00:46:01.294
this is the right thing to do because we
love

00:46:01.294 --> 00:46:03.950
the Afghan people and we want to do the
right thing.

00:46:03.950 --> 00:46:10.240
Even thought that was true, the argument
was that we'll lose if we don't do this.

00:46:10.240 --> 00:46:11.700
This is the only way to succeed.

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:15.060
And that's interesting, because people
drop back and they go, wow!

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:15.440
Really?

00:46:15.440 --> 00:46:16.765
I say, yeah, absolutely!

00:46:16.765 --> 00:46:19.075
And so then you can push cultural change
if

00:46:19.075 --> 00:46:22.660
they think that's what has to happen to
move forward.

00:46:22.660 --> 00:46:23.670
You know the South had to chance.

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:27.570
The Mubarak regime, Egypt, had to change.

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:32.350
But a lot of people, Syria has to change,
but think about it now, there are a

00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:34.667
lot of groups in Syria for whom change

00:46:34.667 --> 00:46:38.700
will be cultural changes as well as
political change.

00:46:38.700 --> 00:46:41.230
So they're holding on very strongly, to be
expected.

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:43.570
So, you gotta make that argument to 'em.

00:46:43.570 --> 00:46:45.800
But then you gotta accept that there's a,
there's a group

00:46:45.800 --> 00:46:49.720
of people who are gonna lose equities in
the near term.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:52.280
And you have to, you kinda have to power
through that.

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:54.990
It's not gonna, most cultural change is
not

00:46:54.990 --> 00:46:58.970
entirely, voluntary on, on groups of
people's parts.

00:46:58.970 --> 00:46:59.630
As you know.

00:46:59.630 --> 00:46:59.959
Thanks, Rachel.

00:46:59.959 --> 00:47:02.395
That's alright.

00:47:02.395 --> 00:47:03.750
Ma'am.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.240
&gt;&gt; General Crystal, thank you so much for
your

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:08.230
service to this country and for being here
today.

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:12.561
My name is Coleen Mizuki and I'm a member
of a, a nascent institute looking

00:47:12.561 --> 00:47:14.554
at innovative ways to, to help our

00:47:14.554 --> 00:47:18.080
veterans after their deployment to a
combat zone.

00:47:18.080 --> 00:47:20.445
And very, I think importantly, I also
volunteer to

00:47:20.445 --> 00:47:24.350
work with soldiers and family members in
the reintegration process.

00:47:24.350 --> 00:47:26.811
So, you talked about, you know, leadership
not

00:47:26.811 --> 00:47:28.978
being complex but not very hard, and I
think

00:47:28.978 --> 00:47:32.200
that, for me, what underlines everything
you've talked about

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:36.089
in, say, counterinsurgency approach in
Afghanistan, is mindset change.

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:41.780
So I wonder if you have some words of
wisdom that

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:45.170
I can pass on to soldiers that I work with
and helping

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:50.850
them understand that after very difficult
experiences in combat, how

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:55.710
do I, how do, how can they accept that
mindset change is possible?

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:59.799
That it's not complex, but it's also not
very easy.

00:47:59.799 --> 00:48:01.003
Thank you.

00:48:01.003 --> 00:48:02.089
&gt;&gt; Why, that's a great point.

00:48:02.089 --> 00:48:03.991
Thanks for what you're doing.

00:48:03.991 --> 00:48:10.426
Yeah mindset is extraordinarily important.

00:48:10.426 --> 00:48:14.050
What you do, why you did it, and what you
did is important.

00:48:14.050 --> 00:48:19.710
And if you think about veterans sometimes,
they don't have a problem going.

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:22.340
But when we ask someone to go into the
military,

00:48:22.340 --> 00:48:24.600
and go to war, what have we really asked
them?

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:28.550
You say, well, we've asked you to take a
job, and do whatever.

00:48:28.550 --> 00:48:31.059
No, we've really asked them to believe.

00:48:31.059 --> 00:48:35.012
We've said, we're asking you to push the I
believe button in

00:48:35.012 --> 00:48:39.513
your nation, in your leadership, and the
fact will take care of you.

00:48:39.513 --> 00:48:42.360
And it's a huge leap of faith for a young
person to go in

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:46.742
the military and go forward to combat,
because the nation says hey, we love you.

00:48:46.742 --> 00:48:49.179
They, they bought into that.

00:48:49.179 --> 00:48:50.932
They believed.

00:48:50.932 --> 00:48:52.665
Now there's the second part of that.

00:48:52.665 --> 00:48:55.322
I used to go around to battlefields and
places

00:48:55.322 --> 00:48:58.500
in DC and what not and I'd see monuments.

00:48:58.500 --> 00:49:01.800
And I'd say, wow, this is a monument to
the ego of the people who were there.

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:04.600
They really just want their, their head
patted

00:49:04.600 --> 00:49:06.910
for the fact they did that and what not.

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:08.770
That's not it at all.

00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:11.950
As I get older I realize, that's the
opposite of it.

00:49:13.140 --> 00:49:17.310
When you go to a monument like the World
War II Veteran's Memorial there in DC.

00:49:18.390 --> 00:49:22.360
What you really see is of the 8 million
Americans who served

00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:25.520
in uniform and then all the families who
were touched by it.

00:49:25.520 --> 00:49:26.580
You know, war is pretty ugly.

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:31.400
When you're up close to it, it doesn't
feel good.

00:49:31.400 --> 00:49:33.470
It doesn't feel clean.

00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:34.430
It's war.

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:35.280
And it's frustrating.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:37.310
It never comes out exactly like you want
it to.

00:49:37.310 --> 00:49:41.944
But at the end of the day, what we've
gotta reassure people and what

00:49:41.944 --> 00:49:47.540
monuments do is they basically tell
people, you did what we asked you to do.

00:49:47.540 --> 00:49:52.538
When the nation asked you to do something,
you did that.

00:49:52.538 --> 00:49:54.338
You stood.

00:49:54.338 --> 00:49:54.953
You served.

00:49:54.953 --> 00:49:55.758
You sacrificed.

00:49:55.758 --> 00:49:57.430
Whatever it is we asked.

00:49:57.430 --> 00:49:59.870
And that's what I think people are lookin
for, they're

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:03.130
lookin for some reassurance that they did
what was asked.

00:50:03.130 --> 00:50:05.692
When we go to veterans now what, what i
think

00:50:05.692 --> 00:50:11.500
we've gotta do particularly, you know
these wars aren't particularly popular.

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:12.790
You know I'm not that excited about them.

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:17.592
But I would tell you what we've gotta do
is convince the young people who suffer

00:50:17.592 --> 00:50:23.070
from different kinds of stress or physical
industries, injuries, hey, thanks.

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:24.420
You did what we asked you to do.

00:50:24.420 --> 00:50:27.490
And that's pretty darn good.

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:31.489
And we don't, that's gotta be totally
separate from all the other things.

00:50:31.489 --> 00:50:33.104
Let me thank everybody for being here
today.

00:50:33.104 --> 00:50:34.087
I really appreciate it.

00:50:34.087 --> 00:50:44.087
[SOUND]

